1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
Counteroffer is an autonomous buyer. It makes vendors compete for what you need.

2
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:10,000
This board is live. A photographer just replied.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,000
Thirty-nine thousand. It counters at thirty-five and a half.

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,000
I'm Divyansh Agrawal. It starts with one sentence.

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:25,000
Wedding photographer, Mumbai, October eighteenth, eight hours, a highlight video, under forty thousand.

6
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:30,000
It turns that into requirements and a budget I can edit.

7
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,000
The maximum is the number it will never cross.

8
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:40,000
Start sourcing, and Firecrawl searches the live web. Six queries, thirty-five candidate sites.

9
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:45,000
It pulls contacts off each site and merges duplicates.

10
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:50,000
The ones it cannot actually reach are rejected, with the reason attached.

11
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:55,000
This campaign already has seven qualified vendors, so I put it to work.

12
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:59,000
Each campaign gets its own inbox.

13
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:05,000
For this recording the vendors are simulated, so no mail leaves the building.

14
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:11,000
Everything after that is real. Replies parsed, quotes normalized, counters sent.

15
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:17,000
On the left, the email as it arrived. On the right, what the agent understood.

16
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:22,500
Line items, taxes, travel, hours, and anything the vendor still has not answered.

17
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:28,000
Frame Co is the cheapest quote in the room, at twenty-seven thousand.

18
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:33,500
It ranks fifth. The quote is seventeen percent complete, and the board explains itself.

19
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:41,500
BudgetClicks opened at thirty thousand. Asked what was actually included, the all-in price came back at forty-three thousand eight hundred.

20
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:46,500
Over the hard budget, so it is out. That limit lives in code.

21
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:52,000
Accepting a deal is the one thing it never does on its own.

22
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:58,500
I choose a finalist, it waits for my approval, and only my click confirms it.

23
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:04,500
Every offer side by side, on the same basis.

24
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:10,000
Close the campaign, and what each vendor did goes into my supplier network.

25
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:14,500
Reply rate, opening against final quote, how far they moved.

26
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:20,500
The next campaign ranks them on how they really quote, not on their marketing.

27
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:26,000
Firecrawl finds the market, OpenAI reads the replies, and Convex remembers all of it.

28
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:30,500
Tell it what you need. Make them compete.
